<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christmas tree</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> tree is a decorated tree, usually an evergreen conifer such as a spruce, pine or fir, set up for the celebration of Christmas. The custom developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> (now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and Latvia), and was first recorded among German Lutherans in the 16th century; records indicate that a tree was placed in the Cathedral of Strasbourg in 1539. Trees were hung with apples, wafers, paper roses and confectionery and lit with candles, which electric lights have often replaced. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the German-born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Queen Charlotte</a> introduced a tree in 1800, and the custom spread more widely after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>'s marriage to Prince Albert.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas-tree.jpg" alt="Christmas tree" width="330" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Warby · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A1_Christmas_Tree_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Christmas tree</b> came from Central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Christmas tree</b> developed in Central Europe, particularly Germany and Livonia, and was first recorded among German Lutherans in the 16th century; in Britain the German-born Queen Charlotte introduced a tree in 1800, and the custom spread after the marriage of Queen Victoria to Prince Albert.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>As the custom of decorating <b>Christmas trees</b> spread in these years, it became one of the best-known Christmas songs in both German and English, although it is not, strictly, about such a tree.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-tannenbaum">O Tannenbaum</a></li></ul></section>
